{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/strict-action-and-revision.md", "content": "# Strict-action discipline after failed submissions

In costly ARC evaluator streams, every turn must contain exactly one allowed JSON action. A failed submission is evidence against that complete candidate, but binary feedback does not identify which part is wrong. Record the exact candidate/rule attempted and do not resend it unchanged. Re-derive a materially different candidate from the demonstration constraints or spend a demo request only when it can distinguish live hypotheses.

Avoid non-puzzle tool calls during the active evaluator loop: they do not provide new constraints and increase the risk of violating the one-action contract. Before responding, check current demo/attempt counts, ensure the selected action is still allowed, and emit only its JSON object on the final line.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/strict-action-and-revision.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/rejected-grid-rule-audit.md", "content": "# Rule audit after a rejected submission

A wrong submission rejects the whole hypothesis, not just one guessed cell. Re-derive from all demo pairs rather than iterating local edits. In cluttered inputs, identify regular object geometry and compare each output cell: isolated cells of other colors within otherwise uniform shapes may be noise or overlays, and the output may normalize the whole object while removing those intrusions. Conversely, do not generalize this without demo support. Audit both the transformed extent and every unusual cell outside it; test the candidate against every demo before spending another attempt.

When a submitted grid is not clearly supported, a further demo can be more valuable than another guess, but specify which competing interpretations it could distinguish. Keep strict protocol: exactly one JSON action per puzzle turn.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/rejected-grid-rule-audit.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/session-lessons-protocol-and-fill-rule.md", "content": "# Protocol discipline and avoiding speculative fill rules

Continual ARC tasks require one valid action on every active turn, even after an interruption. A prose status report is not a valid substitute; when the instance remains active, choose among submit, request_demos, or finish according to the live counts and end-state rules. Do not make unrelated tool calls in the evaluator loop: they consume interaction opportunities without providing puzzle evidence.

After each demo, compare all changed and preserved cells before taking another costly action. In fill/color tasks, do not assume the output is a filled bounding rectangle or the complement of a flood-filled exterior. Those are competing hypotheses that need to explain every demo, including holes, boundaries, marker cells, and untouched regions. Consider directional propagation or selectively filled regions only when supported by cellwise evidence. An incorrect submission should prompt a meaningful rule revision, not a cosmetic redraw.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-reasoning/references/session-lessons-protocol-and-fill-rule.md"}
